  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized small-swing pendulum: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s². This is exact for the model and close to a real pendulum for small swings.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob counts as a point at the string's end.",
    "There is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing amplitude is fixed at 10°. At that angle a real pendulum's period is only about 0.2% longer.",
    "The motion follows the timeline time, so changing the length restarts the swing from the current time. When the 60 s loop wraps around, the swing can jump."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const G=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/G);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{arm:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},stringN:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bobN:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,lenOut:L}};}"
  }
